 is ny_zone\n assert first[0] == day_start\n-assert first[1] == datetime(2025, 11, 2, 1, 30, tzinfo=edt)\n-assert second[0] == datetime(2025, 11, 2, 1, 45, tzinfo=edt)\n+assert first[1].astimezone(UTC) == datetime(2025, 11, 2, 5, 30, tzinfo=UTC)\n+assert second[0].astimezone(UTC) == datetime(2025, 11, 2, 5, 45, tzinfo=UTC)\n assert second[1] == day_end\n total = sum(\n     end.astimezone(UTC) - start.astimezone(UTC) for start, end in gaps\n )\n","additions":2,"deletions":2},"truncated":false},"title":"tests/test_slots.py","time":{"start":1794047244350,"end":1794047244357}},"id":"prt_1b58c6c8c001XhrUehU8x8AMtc","sessionID":"ses_e4a838527ffe8oucTkjfNF4FOG","messageID":"msg_1b58bc9c0001JlmP6uWQqbJghc"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794047244405,"sessionID":"ses_e4a838527ffe8oucTkjfNF4FOG","part":{"id":"prt_1b58c64860014H4NX6jpsWB3Kg","messageID":"msg_1b58bc9c0001JlmP6uWQqbJghc","sessionID":"ses_e4a838527ffe8oucTkjfNF4FOG","type":"text","text":"\n\nThis is a Python 3.12 `zoneinfo` equality bug: two datetimes representing the same UTC instant compare `False` with `==` when one uses `ZoneInfo` and the other uses a fixed-offset `timezone`, even though `a - b` is `0:00:00` and both convert to the same UTC. I'll fix the test to compare by UTC instant (which is the more robust assertion anyway), and also fix the total-duration assertion (window is 25h, busy is 15m, so free is 24h 45m, not 24h 15m).\n\n","time":{"start":1794047239302,"end":1794047244402}}}
